    "terminal_reason": "completed",
    "result_text": "I've implemented `clean_orders` in `app/clean.py`. `pytest -q` passes, but the only existing test checks that the function exists. I checked the actual behavior with a separate script.\n\n**What it does:**\n- **Copy first:** it works on `df.copy()`, so the input frame is never modified.\n- **Text columns:** it strips leading and trailing whitespace, then turns empty strings into missing values. This includes cells that were only whitespace, like `\"  \"`. It looks for text columns under both the `\"string\"` and `\"object\"` types. That matters because the pinned pandas 3.0.6 stores CSV text in its new `str` type rather than `object`, which I confirmed in the pandas docs.\n  - For `str` columns it uses the built-in `.str.strip()`.\n  - For `object` columns it only changes values that are actually strings. Non-string values like numbers or `None` stay as they are, whereas `.str.strip()` would have turned them into NaN.\n- **`qty`:** missing values are filled with 0.\n- **Everything else** (such as `price`) is left alone.\n\n**What I checked:** I loaded a small CSV through `load_orders`.\n- `\" A1 \"` became `\"A1\"`, `\" hi \"` became `\"hi\"`, and blank or whitespace-only cells became NaN.\n- An empty `qty` became `0.0`.\n- The input frame was identical afterwards.\n- An `object` column containing `[\" x \", 5, \"\", None]` came out as `[\"x\", 5, NaN, None]`.\n\n**One thing to note:** when `qty` has missing values, pandas reads it as floats (`float64`), so filled values appear as `0.0` and the column stays float. I didn't convert it to integers, because you asked to leave other values as they are.\n\n**Cleanup needed:** the check script `scratch_check.py` is still in the project root. The permission settings blocked me from deleting it, so please remove it."
  },
